values ('bce0f9e6-5a52-470b-a29f-4edfd4b91ca0', '4efdcd38-f5e6-4f15-ac4f-76d8788c8053', 'variables-and-types', 'Values, variables, and types', 'Store information in named variables and learn which type each value has.', 'beginner', 25, 1, '["Create variables with meaningful names","Distinguish strings, integers, floats, and booleans","Predict the type of a value","Reassign a variable and explain what changes"]'::jsonb, '{"intro":"A program works by keeping track of values. A variable is a name attached to a value, and Python infers the value''s type from the value itself.","sections":[{"title":"Assignment","paragraphs":["The equals sign assigns a value to a name. The name is on the left, the value is evaluated on the right, and only then is it stored.","Names are case-sensitive. `score` and `Score` are different variables. Choose descriptive names: `remaining_attempts` is clearer than `x`."],"code":"learner_name = \"Ada\"\ncompleted_lessons = 3\naccuracy = 0.875\nis_enrolled = True"},{"title":"Types matter","paragraphs":["`\"42\"` is text. `42` is an integer, and `42.0` is a float. They may look similar when printed, but they behave differently: you can add numbers, but `\"42\" + 1` raises a TypeError.","Use `type(value)` when you need to inspect a value. A boolean is either `True` or `False`; Python capitalizes both."],"code":"print(type(\"42\"))    # <class ''str''>\nprint(type(42))      # <class ''int''>\nprint(type(42.0))    # <class ''float''>\nprint(type(True))    # <class ''bool''>"},{"title":"Reassignment","paragraphs":["A variable can point at a new value later. Reassigning does not mutate the old value; it changes which value the name refers to."],"code":"attempts = 1\nattempts = attempts + 1\nprint(attempts)  # 2"}],"keyPoints":["A variable is a name bound to a value.","Python infers types; you can inspect them with type().","Use descriptive names, and remember names are case-sensitive."]}'::jsonb, true)
