values ('acb595d7-aaa3-47ea-a3c8-37b48676bc8b', '4efdcd38-f5e6-4f15-ac4f-76d8788c8053', 'strings-and-output', 'Strings, formatting, and output', 'Clean text, combine strings, and produce readable output with f-strings.', 'beginner', 30, 2, '["Use string methods such as strip, lower, and replace","Build readable output with f-strings","Write a function that returns a value instead of printing it"]'::jsonb, '{"intro":"Most useful programs do something with text: names, messages, labels, file contents, or user input. Strings are immutable, so methods return new strings rather than changing the original.","sections":[{"title":"Useful string methods","paragraphs":["`strip()` removes whitespace from both ends. `lower()` and `upper()` change case. `replace(old, new)` substitutes text.","These methods do not modify the original string. If you want to keep the result, assign it or return it."],"code":"raw = \"  Hello, World  \"\ncleaned = raw.strip().lower()\nprint(cleaned)          # hello, world\nprint(raw)              # \"  Hello, World  \""},{"title":"f-strings","paragraphs":["An f-string embeds expressions directly in text. It is the clearest way to combine labels and values."],"code":"first = \"Ada\"\nlessons = 12\nprint(f\"{first} has completed {lessons} lessons.\")"},{"title":"Return, don''t print","paragraphs":["A function that returns a string can be tested, reused, and composed. A function that only prints is harder to test because the output is not a value."],"code":"def label(name):\n    return name.strip().title()\n\nprint(label(\"  aDA \"))  # Ada"}],"keyPoints":["Strings are immutable; methods produce new strings.","f-strings interpolate expressions directly.","Prefer returning values over printing inside reusable functions."]}'::jsonb, true)
