values ('4618483d-b6cb-467f-a8a6-85f26fa3552e', '4efdcd38-f5e6-4f15-ac4f-76d8788c8053', 'booleans-and-conditionals', 'Booleans and conditional logic', 'Make decisions with comparisons, if statements, and clear branch conditions.', 'beginner', 35, 3, '["Use comparison and logical operators","Write if, elif, and else branches","Handle invalid input with an explicit branch","Explain the order in which conditions are evaluated"]'::jsonb, '{"intro":"Conditional logic lets a program choose a path. Python reads branches from top to bottom and runs the first branch whose condition is true.","sections":[{"title":"Comparisons and truth","paragraphs":["Comparisons produce booleans. `==` checks equality, while `=` assigns a value. Combine conditions with `and`, `or`, and `not`.","Python treats several values as falsy: `False`, `None`, `0`, `\"\"`, and empty collections. Be explicit when the difference matters."],"code":"score = 72\nprint(score >= 60)             # True\nprint(score >= 90 or score < 0)  # False\nprint(not score == 0)            # True"},{"title":"Branch order","paragraphs":["The first matching branch wins. Put the most specific checks before the general fallback, and make the fallback an `else` rather than a separate condition when possible."],"code":"def label(score):\n    if score < 0 or score > 100:\n        return \"invalid\"\n    if score >= 90:\n        return \"strong\"\n    if score >= 60:\n        return \"passing\"\n    return \"needs practice\""}],"keyPoints":["Comparisons return booleans; == is not =.","Only the first true branch executes.","Validate the edges before the normal range."]}'::jsonb, true)
