values ('ed6304a1-7bc0-4fa3-a972-cf3918777b83', '4efdcd38-f5e6-4f15-ac4f-76d8788c8053', 'functions-and-parameters', 'Functions, parameters, and reuse', 'Design small functions with clear inputs, return values, defaults, and deliberate errors.', 'intermediate', 40, 7, '["Define a function with parameters and a return value","Use default parameter values","Raise ValueError for invalid input","Split a larger problem into named helpers"]'::jsonb, '{"intro":"Functions turn a repeated idea into a named piece of behavior. A good function has a clear job, accepts the data it needs, and returns a useful result instead of hiding output in print.","sections":[{"title":"Parameters are inputs","paragraphs":["Parameters are names the function uses for the values passed by the caller. Return sends a value back to the caller. A function without an explicit return returns None."],"code":"def area(width, height):\n    return width * height\n\nprint(area(3, 4))  # 12"},{"title":"Defaults","paragraphs":["A default makes an argument optional. Put parameters with defaults after parameters without them. Avoid mutable defaults such as [] because the same object is reused across calls."],"code":"def greet(name, punctuation=\"!\"):\n    return f\"Hello, {name}{punctuation}\"\n\nprint(greet(\"Ada\"))       # Hello, Ada!\nprint(greet(\"Ada\", \".\"))  # Hello, Ada."},{"title":"Validate deliberately","paragraphs":["When a function receives input it cannot sensibly process, raise a specific error with a helpful message. This makes the contract clear and prevents a confusing failure later."],"code":"def percentage(part, whole):\n    if whole == 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"whole must not be zero\")\n    return part / whole * 100"}],"keyPoints":["A return value is easier to test than printed output.","Default parameters make inputs optional.","Validate inputs where the function can detect a real problem.","Small helpers with clear names make decomposition possible."]}'::jsonb, true)
