values ('ea6b4877-d6bf-4c32-ab69-60975bf54803', '4efdcd38-f5e6-4f15-ac4f-76d8788c8053', 'files-and-exceptions', 'Files and deliberate exceptions', 'Read and write files safely, use context managers, and handle failures without hiding them.', 'intermediate', 45, 9, '["Open a file with a with statement","Read lines and convert text to numbers","Raise and catch specific exceptions","Avoid broad except blocks that hide bugs"]'::jsonb, '{"intro":"Files let a program outlive a single run. Exceptions are how Python reports that an operation could not be completed. Used well, they make failures explicit rather than silent.","sections":[{"title":"Context managers","paragraphs":["A `with open(...) as file` block closes the file even if an error occurs. Use an explicit encoding for text files so behavior is predictable across systems."],"code":"with open(\"scores.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n    file.write(\"10\\n20\\n\")\n\nwith open(\"scores.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n    for line in file:\n        print(line.strip())"},{"title":"Catch what you can handle","paragraphs":["Catch the narrow exception you expect and can recover from. If you cannot do anything useful, let the error propagate with context."],"code":"def safe_divide(a, b):\n    try:\n        return a / b\n    except ZeroDivisionError:\n        return None\n\nprint(safe_divide(6, 0))  # None"},{"title":"Raise with meaning","paragraphs":["Use `raise ValueError(\"message\")` when a caller supplies data that violates the function''s contract. Include enough detail to diagnose the problem without exposing secrets."],"code":"def require_positive(value):\n    if value <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"value must be greater than zero\")\n    return value"}],"keyPoints":["with open(...) guarantees the file is closed.","Catch specific exceptions and only when you can recover.","Use raise to enforce a function''s contract.","File data is text until you convert it."]}'::jsonb, true)
